WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.580 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.340 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.500 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:22.810 align:middle line:90%


00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:23.560 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.910 align:middle line:90%
Welcome back.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:31.180 align:middle line:84%
We're going to go through a very
specific example of association

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.540 align:middle line:84%
studying, illustrating this
extremely important statistic,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:39.160 align:middle line:84%
the chi-square statistic
in the simplest case.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:46.090 align:middle line:84%
That simple case will be a
single allele combination,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:52.390 align:middle line:84%
two alleles and two possible
phenotypic outcomes,

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:57.310 align:middle line:84%
HIV resistance and
HIV non-resistance.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.280 align:middle line:84%
So to set up this
association study, which

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.790 align:middle line:84%
would be computational,
let's talk a little bit

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.989 align:middle line:90%
about the biology here.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:08.890 align:middle line:90%


00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.550 align:middle line:84%
All viruses need to get
into your cells somehow.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.440 align:middle line:84%
HIV has a number of
proteins to which it binds

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.610 align:middle line:90%
on the surface of your cell.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.210 align:middle line:84%
These proteins are not-- they're
not designed to bind HIV.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.480 align:middle line:90%
They do something else.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.810 align:middle line:84%
This is in a family of
chemokine receptors.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.960 align:middle line:84%
This is involved in
intracellular signaling,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.360 align:middle line:84%
and so it's a receptor
normally for these chemokines.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.080 align:middle line:84%
But it is also a
receptor for the virus.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.510 align:middle line:84%
And in the human population,
as we alluded to earlier,

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.830 align:middle line:84%
there are at least two
alleles, two common alleles.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:52.720 align:middle line:84%
One of them, the top
one, capital CCR5,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.220 align:middle line:84%
has this long,
open reading frame.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.190 align:middle line:84%
And this you could translate
by your pearl strip

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.080 align:middle line:84%
that you wrote for
the problem set.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.450 align:middle line:84%
And the DNA sequences
in the middle here

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.910 align:middle line:84%
and the protein sequence derived
from it is on the top strand.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.789 align:middle line:90%
And that's the capital CCR5.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:13.900 align:middle line:84%
And the little delta or
deletion Ccr5 is below it.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.230 align:middle line:84%
You've locked out 32
base pairs, which is not

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:18.700 align:middle line:90%
an interval multiple of three.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.670 align:middle line:84%
And as you know
from last lecture,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.980 align:middle line:84%
that means it's going to be
a different genetic code.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.190 align:middle line:84%
At every point downstream, this
is going to be frame-shifted,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.480 align:middle line:84%
and so you get a whole
new set of amino acids

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.400 align:middle line:84%
for the entirety of the rest
of the carboxy terminus.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.630 align:middle line:84%
So this in black
here-- so we're showing

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.730 align:middle line:84%
here in the single-letter code
a somewhat realistic folding

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.910 align:middle line:84%
of the-- schematic folding
of the protein, showing here

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.340 align:middle line:90%
disulfide between two cysteines.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.000 align:middle line:84%
Transmembrane region, we
have these hydrophobic alpha

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:52.840 align:middle line:90%
helices.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.780 align:middle line:84%
And finally, in the
black amino acids,

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.120 align:middle line:84%
the C terminus, the
end of the protein

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.570 align:middle line:84%
are all substituted in
this deletion mutant.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:06.880 align:middle line:84%
Now that is sufficient to
cause resistance to the virus.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.370 align:middle line:84%
It probably has some
effect on its ability

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.490 align:middle line:84%
to bind chemokines or react
to them in some other way.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:17.170 align:middle line:90%
And presumably, this is not--

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.140 align:middle line:84%
there is some deleterious
effect we do not know about.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.215 align:middle line:84%
But in any case,
we can assess this.

00:03:23.215 --> 00:03:25.450 align:middle line:84%
We can make a genetic
assay for this.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:30.070 align:middle line:84%
And we can look in
human populations

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.870 align:middle line:84%
for their resistance to HIV and
for the presence of either two

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:41.440 align:middle line:84%
alleles of the resistance or
two alleles of the susceptible.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm kind of biasing
you here in this.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.323 align:middle line:84%
We haven't done the
association study.

00:03:45.323 --> 00:03:47.740 align:middle line:84%
I really shouldn't be referring
to it as resistant or not,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.350 align:middle line:84%
but I think it helps
you visualize it.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.460 align:middle line:84%
We'll be rigorous enough
in the next slide.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:57.310 align:middle line:84%
So here is, if you will,
the big allele, right?

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.590 align:middle line:90%
It's the original.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.130 align:middle line:90%
It's the non-deleted allele.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.560 align:middle line:84%
So when you do a PCR
assay where you're

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.100 align:middle line:84%
amplifying with two primers
across the region that

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.200 align:middle line:84%
could be deleted
or not, you'll tend

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.360 align:middle line:84%
to get a large
amplification product.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.170 align:middle line:84%
You prime synthesis
until you get something

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.355 align:middle line:90%
which has 403 base pairs.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.355 align:middle line:84%
And there's enough of it
that you can display it

00:04:18.355 --> 00:04:21.100 align:middle line:84%
on electrophoretic gel,
and it migrates slowly

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.320 align:middle line:90%
because it's large.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.110 align:middle line:84%
If you have a homozygote
for the deletion allele--

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.400 align:middle line:84%
we'll not call it the HIV
resistance allele just yet--

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.200 align:middle line:84%
then you'll get a
371-base-pair PCR product.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you have
the heterozygote,

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.090 align:middle line:84%
you'll get both, the large and
the small allele represented

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.560 align:middle line:90%
on this electrophoretic assay.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.820 align:middle line:84%
A very simple, very robust
DNA PCR-based assay.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.320 align:middle line:90%


00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:55.430 align:middle line:84%
So now let's ask whether
one allele or the other

00:04:55.430 --> 00:05:00.680 align:middle line:84%
is more abundant in people
which are observed to be

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.660 align:middle line:90%
seropositive or seronegative.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Seropositive means
that they have

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.520 align:middle line:84%
circulating antibodies
in their serum

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.050 align:middle line:84%
which react positively
to the HIV virus.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:17.160 align:middle line:84%
And so that's not
necessarily, but is commonly

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.420 align:middle line:84%
associated with being heavily
exposed to the HIV virus,

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.700 align:middle line:84%
and therefore
basically infected.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.320 align:middle line:84%
Seronegative means
that you are--

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.510 align:middle line:84%
it's an indication
that you're resistant.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:33.635 align:middle line:84%
Now we could do this as
a two-by-two matrix here

00:05:33.635 --> 00:05:37.190 align:middle line:84%
of alleles versus
outcomes, or we

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:39.440 align:middle line:84%
could do it as a
three-by-two where we

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.380 align:middle line:90%
have genotypes versus outcomes.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.430 align:middle line:84%
The three genotypes are
big-big, little-little,

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.065 align:middle line:90%
and big-little heterozygote.

00:05:48.065 --> 00:05:50.690 align:middle line:84%
But let's just do it-- just keep
it simple in terms of alleles.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.880 align:middle line:90%
If there is a selective advan--

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.860 align:middle line:84%
or there's a
perceivable association

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.790 align:middle line:84%
between the seropositive,
seronegative, and the allele,

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:05.355 align:middle line:84%
you should be able to see it
in both the genotype or just

00:06:05.355 --> 00:06:05.855 align:middle line:90%
the allele.

00:06:05.855 --> 00:06:07.010 align:middle line:90%
The allele is simpler.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:08.540 align:middle line:90%
It's a two-by-two matrix.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:13.220 align:middle line:84%
And so these are the data,
just these four boxes here.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:20.750 align:middle line:84%
The big allele is 1,278 observed
negative and 1,368 observed

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.810 align:middle line:90%
positive for a total of 2,646.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.480 align:middle line:84%
You can see that
there are fewer total

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.057 align:middle line:84%
in the population surveyed
of the deletion mutant.

00:06:30.057 --> 00:06:32.390 align:middle line:84%
This is consistent with the
claim that I made all along.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.240 align:middle line:84%
It's about 9% in general
human population,

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.235 align:middle line:90%
and it's 9% of this population.

00:06:37.235 --> 00:06:39.240 align:middle line:90%
That's good.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.880 align:middle line:84%
But now you have to
correct for that.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.120 align:middle line:84%
You need to-- you
can't just ask,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.590 align:middle line:90%
is it more frequent here or not?

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.110 align:middle line:84%
You have to correct for
the actual frequencies

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:48.890 align:middle line:90%
in the population.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:55.220 align:middle line:84%
And the way that's done is you
calculate another table, which

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:01.820 align:middle line:84%
is the expected number of each
of these combinations of big

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.680 align:middle line:84%
with seronegative,
big with seropositive,

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.870 align:middle line:84%
and so forth, under
the assumption

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.835 align:middle line:90%
that it's completely random.

00:07:07.835 --> 00:07:10.220 align:middle line:84%
You know the allele
frequencies in the population,

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.625 align:middle line:84%
but say that they just randomly
associated with whether it's

00:07:13.625 --> 00:07:15.170 align:middle line:90%
seropositive or seronegative.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.780 align:middle line:84%
You use these totals
here and the frequencies

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:21.530 align:middle line:84%
in the population to
generate this expected end

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:22.700 align:middle line:90%
of the random assumption.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.790 align:middle line:84%
And then you look for
deviation of the real ones

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.505 align:middle line:84%
of the observed
versus the expected.

00:07:27.505 --> 00:07:34.990 align:middle line:90%


00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:39.310 align:middle line:84%
So any deviation between the
expected and the observed

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:40.900 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
significant in this.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.490 align:middle line:90%


00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.720 align:middle line:84%
So you want to do is take the
difference between the observed

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:47.430 align:middle line:90%
and expected.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.840 align:middle line:84%
So we're working on
a statistic here,

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.120 align:middle line:84%
a type of measure
that will determine,

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.480 align:middle line:84%
how far from expectation
are the observations

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.750 align:middle line:84%
on the left-hand side
in this two-by-two?

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:02.140 align:middle line:84%
And so for every square
in the two-by-two,

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.780 align:middle line:84%
you find a corresponding
expected number

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:05.920 align:middle line:90%
and you subtract it.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:07.210 align:middle line:90%
That's the starting point.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.870 align:middle line:84%
But you don't care whether
it's negative or positive.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.380 align:middle line:90%
You want to make it positive.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.978 align:middle line:84%
So the trick we'll often use
is to take the square of that.

00:08:14.978 --> 00:08:16.520 align:middle line:84%
So you could take
the absolute value.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.630 align:middle line:84%
You take the square for the
chi-square, oddly enough.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:23.470 align:middle line:84%
And then you want to put it
on some kind of standard scale

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:27.040 align:middle line:84%
so that when you do a chi-square
for any kind of phenomenon,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.240 align:middle line:90%
you'll be able to compare.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.870 align:middle line:84%
And the way you do that is you
divide by the expected number.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:36.400 align:middle line:84%
Or as this puts it on, if
these numbers were really huge,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.530 align:middle line:90%
this would bring them down.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.870 align:middle line:84%
Very small, it'll
bring the same point.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.320 align:middle line:84%
And so you take the sum over
all the squares in here,

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.260 align:middle line:90%
and that's 15.6.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:53.150 align:middle line:84%
And that's saying that it
deviates from the expectation

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:58.640 align:middle line:84%
by this amount normalized as
a fraction of the expected.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.980 align:middle line:84%
Now to determine just a little
sidelight on the chi-square,

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:07.400 align:middle line:84%
in order to determine
the probability of 15.6

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:08.810 align:middle line:84%
being significant,
what does that

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.762 align:middle line:90%
mean, looking at this at 15.6?

00:09:10.762 --> 00:09:12.470 align:middle line:84%
You want to turn this
into a probability,

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:15.140 align:middle line:84%
because probabilities are the
common language that we can all

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:19.430 align:middle line:84%
share our surprise at this
being different from expected,

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.255 align:middle line:84%
the null hypothesis that
this is the same as expected.

00:09:22.255 --> 00:09:23.630 align:middle line:84%
And so in order
to evaluate that,

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.850 align:middle line:84%
you have to ask how
much freedom there--

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.030 align:middle line:90%
degrees of freedom.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:32.290 align:middle line:84%
This is jargon for,
how many different ways

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.010 align:middle line:90%
can these two numbers vary?

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, since we know
the total number,

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:44.120 align:middle line:84%
when we observe the number of
CCR5, that fixes in the columns

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.800 align:middle line:90%
the number of deletions, right?

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:49.250 align:middle line:84%
Because it's just going to be
the number of big alleles taken

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:50.730 align:middle line:90%
from the total.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.910 align:middle line:84%
So in a way, the degrees
of freedom is just one.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:57.350 align:middle line:84%
You fixed this one number, and
the other one is now known.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.430 align:middle line:84%
And the same thing's true
for rows and columns.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.620 align:middle line:90%
So the rows minus--

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.110 align:middle line:84%
it's just one and
the columns is one.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.940 align:middle line:84%
And so the whole
degrees of freedom

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.910 align:middle line:84%
is the product that rows
and columns, which is one.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:08.840 align:middle line:90%
So you plug this in.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.760 align:middle line:84%
If you look in your
standard statistical--

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.600 align:middle line:84%
or your favorite statistical
book or software package, Excel

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.420 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, you plug
in this chi-square value

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.370 align:middle line:84%
and this degrees of freedom,
and you get this probability,

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:21.890 align:middle line:90%
which is very significant.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:27.110 align:middle line:84%
Generally better than 5%
means that you will only

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.430 align:middle line:90%
be wrong 20% of the time.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.120 align:middle line:90%
Some people would prefer to--

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.330 align:middle line:84%
this means you'll be wrong eight
times in 10,000 or something

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:36.830 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:39.470 align:middle line:90%
Very, very infrequently.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.640 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.480 align:middle line:90%
Now this is great.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:43.730 align:middle line:90%
This is a two-by-two matrix.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:45.290 align:middle line:90%
We found an association.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.080 align:middle line:84%
We have a plausible
molecular mechanism

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.640 align:middle line:90%
in the previous slide.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:53.330 align:middle line:84%
But how was it that
we pulled CCR5 out

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:54.830 align:middle line:90%
of-- this rabbit out of the hat?

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:57.260 align:middle line:90%
I mean, why CCR5?

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:00.350 align:middle line:84%
There are 40,000-some
genes in the human genome.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:00.920 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:01.795 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What is it?

00:11:01.795 --> 00:11:05.960 align:middle line:84%
I was curious as to,
what population is it?

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.232 align:middle line:84%
I mean, in which there's
slightly more seropositive

00:11:11.232 --> 00:11:12.190 align:middle line:90%
or seronegative people.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:14.944 align:middle line:90%
This must be a particular--

00:11:14.944 --> 00:11:17.230 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Oh, yeah.

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.120 align:middle line:84%
This is a case
control study where

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.000 align:middle line:84%
you try to get a roughly
equal number of negatives

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.450 align:middle line:84%
and positives that are matched
for socioeconomic group

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:27.820 align:middle line:84%
and race and gender
and things like that.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.330 align:middle line:90%
So that's a setup.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:35.620 align:middle line:84%
But then the-- if there's not
a huge risk ratio for the two

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:38.280 align:middle line:84%
alleles, then you'll expect them
to be close to the population

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.030 align:middle line:84%
frequency, which means
that you can't just

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.490 align:middle line:84%
look at it on inspection
and say, oh, yeah.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.280 align:middle line:84%
All the seropositive
are the big allele.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.160 align:middle line:84%
We wouldn't need a
chi-square at that point.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.950 align:middle line:84%
But this is pretty close to
the population frequency,

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:55.950 align:middle line:84%
and so that's why we
had to do a chi-square.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:03.180 align:middle line:84%
But the question
that we're taking now

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.630 align:middle line:90%
is, how did we pick CCR5?

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.190 align:middle line:84%
And I introduced this as
being a putative receptor

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.032 align:middle line:84%
for the virus, but one of
the first pieces of evidence

00:12:11.032 --> 00:12:12.990 align:middle line:84%
that this was a putative
receptor for the virus

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.700 align:middle line:90%
was this association study.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:19.332 align:middle line:84%
So how was it that it hit
the chemokine receptor?

00:12:19.332 --> 00:12:21.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, you could say, well,
because some kind of hunch

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.030 align:middle line:84%
about chemokines being
involved in immunology,

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:27.720 align:middle line:84%
and immunology being
important in fighting viruses,

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.360 align:middle line:84%
but that wouldn't suggest
that it's an actual receptor.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:33.510 align:middle line:90%


00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.150 align:middle line:90%
I think these are--

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.080 align:middle line:84%
there's all kinds of
inspired guesswork.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.005 align:middle line:84%
There's biochemistry happening
behind the scene, and so on.

00:12:40.005 --> 00:12:42.175 align:middle line:84%
But let's just put that
aside for the moment

00:12:42.175 --> 00:12:43.800 align:middle line:84%
and take the more
general case that you

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.440 align:middle line:84%
wanted to test not just
this one hypothesis,

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.110 align:middle line:84%
that CCR5 is involved,
but you've implicitly

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.922 align:middle line:84%
then tested or explicitly
tested every gene.

00:12:52.922 --> 00:12:54.630 align:middle line:84%
You've gone through
every gene and you've

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.750 align:middle line:84%
taken either the most
common alleles or you've

00:12:57.750 --> 00:12:59.340 align:middle line:84%
sequenced your own
genome and you've

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.615 align:middle line:84%
found the alleles that you have,
whether they're common or rare.

00:13:02.615 --> 00:13:03.240 align:middle line:90%
You don't care.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:06.060 align:middle line:84%
And you want to ask,
what's the association?

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.850 align:middle line:90%


00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:15.200 align:middle line:84%
Let's consider the upper
right-hand panel here

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:20.660 align:middle line:84%
where we have some risk ratio,
a fairly subtle one, 1.5.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.300 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we were talking
about risk ratios of 75

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:24.350 align:middle line:90%
in the case of autism.

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:25.370 align:middle line:90%
This is just 1.5.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:29.390 align:middle line:84%
Very subtle risk ratio, just
like I think in the last one

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:31.250 align:middle line:84%
could have been a
subtle risk ratio.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.930 align:middle line:90%


00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:38.465 align:middle line:84%
And the x-axis is going to
be the number of alleles

00:13:38.465 --> 00:13:40.580 align:middle line:84%
that you're
hypothetically testing.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:42.500 align:middle line:84%
This could be related
to the number of genes

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.480 align:middle line:84%
that you're testing,
or it could be

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.040 align:middle line:84%
more than the number of
genes that you're testing,

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.080 align:middle line:84%
because you can have more
than one allele per gene

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:49.247 align:middle line:90%
that you might want to test.

00:13:49.247 --> 00:13:52.400 align:middle line:84%
Is this allele of this gene
important in this disease,

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:53.645 align:middle line:90%
or is this other one?

00:13:53.645 --> 00:13:55.730 align:middle line:84%
For example, sickle
cell is important,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:59.240 align:middle line:84%
but various other hemoglobin
mutations are not.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.910 align:middle line:84%
This chemokine deletion is
important and maybe another one

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:02.630 align:middle line:90%
isn't.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.740 align:middle line:84%
Many chemokine mutations
will be neutral.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:06.240 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.010 align:middle line:84%
So as you increase the number
of genes and alleles from--

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:12.680 align:middle line:90%
here's 10 to the fourth on up--

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.920 align:middle line:90%
then the number of sib pairs--

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:20.570 align:middle line:84%
so this is a simulation covering
all kinds of experiments

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.600 align:middle line:84%
that you could do
where you can use

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.460 align:middle line:84%
computational
methods to help guide

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:27.860 align:middle line:90%
the design of experiments.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:29.450 align:middle line:84%
If you did an
expensive experiment

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:33.320 align:middle line:84%
and you just happened to
use too few patients and too

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.575 align:middle line:84%
many alleles, then you may
have misused your resources.

00:14:36.575 --> 00:14:39.110 align:middle line:84%
You should have maybe done
fewer alleles and more patients

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.790 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:43.610 align:middle line:84%
And this provides
some guidance here.

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.290 align:middle line:84%
But you can see
that in order to get

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.270 align:middle line:84%
to a very large
number of alleles,

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:48.950 align:middle line:84%
you need a fairly
modest increase

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.040 align:middle line:90%
in the number of patients.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:54.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's due to
the exponential term

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:59.160 align:middle line:84%
that you have in these
probability distributions.

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:01.130 align:middle line:84%
So you actually--
but nevertheless,

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:06.620 align:middle line:84%
it's a big deal cost-wise
going up from, say,

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:11.120 align:middle line:90%
400 patients to 1,600.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:13.970 align:middle line:84%
Nevertheless, it's
only linear in patients

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:18.440 align:middle line:84%
while it's exponential in the
number of alleles you can test,

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:20.470 align:middle line:90%
so that's the good news.

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.220 align:middle line:84%
And you can see some
of these other panels

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:26.570 align:middle line:84%
show the effect of
varying other parameters.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.490 align:middle line:84%
For example, here
on the left you've

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:32.480 align:middle line:84%
got varying the population
frequency of the allele.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.630 align:middle line:84%
As you get to very,
very rare alleles

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:39.890 align:middle line:84%
on the right hand of the
horizontal axis here, then

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:44.210 align:middle line:90%
the number of sib pairs--

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.010 align:middle line:84%
brothers-sisters,
brother-brothers

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:48.200 align:middle line:84%
that you need-- starts
to go through the roof.

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:53.060 align:middle line:84%
And now it's exponential
on both axes,

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.370 align:middle line:84%
or that is to say that
it's a direct relationship.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:55.910 align:middle line:90%
Question?

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:57.628 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's that
z value over there?

00:15:57.628 --> 00:15:58.420 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Hmm?

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's that
z value of over there?

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:05.295 align:middle line:84%
GEORGE CHURCH: Oh, that's
the population frequency that

00:16:05.295 --> 00:16:05.795 align:middle line:90%
you're--

00:16:05.795 --> 00:16:06.994 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: On the bottom?

00:16:06.994 --> 00:16:07.940 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: Hmm?

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:12.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, on-- on the one we were
just talking about on the left,

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:15.320 align:middle line:84%
the population frequency
is the horizontal axis,

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:20.660 align:middle line:84%
and it varies from near unity
to 10 to the minus nine.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.960 align:middle line:84%
It's a very rare
allele frequency.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:27.740 align:middle line:84%
On the right, the one we
started with, the top right,

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:30.980 align:middle line:84%
we just picked 0.5,
which would be very--

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:35.820 align:middle line:84%
be around here on the left-hand
end of the left-hand quadrant.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.480 align:middle line:84%
So you pick one of these
allele frequencies, a fairly--

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.430 align:middle line:84%
where it's equal
frequency of the two

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:43.050 align:middle line:84%
alleles in the
population as where you

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:44.300 align:middle line:90%
do the rest of the simulation.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:46.730 align:middle line:84%
You can think of
all these panels

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:48.470 align:middle line:84%
could be some
multi-dimensional display,

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:51.230 align:middle line:84%
but I wanted to take
them out one at a time.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:54.710 align:middle line:84%
And actually, I did
all these in Excel

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:58.630 align:middle line:84%
using the equations that are
present in this reference

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:01.070 align:middle line:90%
that's given in the slides here.

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:04.530 align:middle line:90%


00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:06.300 align:middle line:84%
A good sign of a
well-written paper

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.609 align:middle line:84%
is for a sort of
average individual

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:11.500 align:middle line:90%
to be able to reproduce it.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So we have how many--
so we've been talking

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:19.040 align:middle line:90%
about new polymorphisms.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:21.290 align:middle line:84%
In fact, the question that
came up during the break

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:28.069 align:middle line:84%
was, if selection and drift
cause allele frequencies to fix

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:31.580 align:middle line:84%
fairly rapidly, drift
in small populations,

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.530 align:middle line:84%
normal-sized
populations will drift

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.833 align:middle line:84%
in fairly short evolutionary
times and selection

00:17:37.833 --> 00:17:40.250 align:middle line:84%
if you have a high selection
coefficient of cost fixation.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.140 align:middle line:84%
Why do we have any allele
frequencies at all?

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:46.640 align:middle line:84%
Why isn't everything fixed at
100% for one particular allele?

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.170 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is mutation.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.578 align:middle line:84%
And where all these new
mutations or polymorphisms

00:17:50.578 --> 00:17:52.370 align:middle line:84%
come from-- actually,
these should probably

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:54.470 align:middle line:84%
be called mutations,
because most of them

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.780 align:middle line:90%
have frequencies less than 1%.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:59.883 align:middle line:84%
Well, here's a specific model
which doesn't follow all

00:17:59.883 --> 00:18:02.300 align:middle line:84%
the assumptions we had before,
but we'll use a different--

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:04.633 align:middle line:84%
we'll use a back-of-the-envelope
calculation to give you

00:18:04.633 --> 00:18:08.150 align:middle line:84%
a feeling for what
is true for the--

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:10.920 align:middle line:84%
what is likely to be the case
for the human population.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.310 align:middle line:84%
So the human population,
there's a little bit

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:16.300 align:middle line:84%
of unknown in some
of these parameters,

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.800 align:middle line:84%
so you should take these all
with a grain of salt. Actually,

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.220 align:middle line:84%
everything I say
you should take.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:24.470 align:middle line:84%
But the number of
populations that we've

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:28.220 align:middle line:84%
had since a bottleneck
in the human population--

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:31.340 align:middle line:84%
maybe there were as few
as 10 to the fourth humans

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:33.620 align:middle line:90%
at some point or another.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:35.300 align:middle line:90%
Maybe less.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.210 align:middle line:84%
But since that time, there have
been about 5,000 generations.

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:39.890 align:middle line:84%
And during that
time, our population

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.140 align:middle line:84%
has now grown to
six billion people.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.710 align:middle line:90%
That's the n prime nn.

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:51.783 align:middle line:84%
And the mutation rate, as
mentioned earlier in response

00:18:51.783 --> 00:18:53.450 align:middle line:84%
to a question, is
around 10 to the minus

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:59.580 align:middle line:84%
eighth per base pair per
genome per generation.

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:02.270 align:middle line:84%
And so the genome size
is about six billion.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.430 align:middle line:84%
Coincidentally, the same
as the population size.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.380 align:middle line:90%
Total coincidence.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.830 align:middle line:84%
Then 10 to the minus eight
times that number of base pairs

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:12.320 align:middle line:84%
means you have
about 60 mutations

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:17.310 align:middle line:90%
or so occurring in a generation.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:20.050 align:middle line:84%
So you've got a steady
flow of new mutations.

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:21.620 align:middle line:84%
Now if you take
that-- just roughly

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:23.930 align:middle line:84%
speaking, if you take that
over 5,000 generations,

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:26.630 align:middle line:90%
you've got 60 times 5,000.

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.490 align:middle line:90%
You've got a very large number--

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.700 align:middle line:84%
on the order of 10 to
the fourth mutations

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:33.630 align:middle line:84%
that have accumulated
in our population.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.890 align:middle line:84%
Assuming relatively
little drift because

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.560 align:middle line:84%
of this exponentially growing
population and relatively

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.830 align:middle line:84%
little selection, then
this is the number

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.240 align:middle line:90%
that might have accumulated.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:48.920 align:middle line:84%
You can do subtle corrections
for this exponential growth.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:51.993 align:middle line:84%
The number of mutations
you got at the beginning

00:19:51.993 --> 00:19:54.410 align:middle line:84%
will have higher frequency,
but there'll be fewer of them,

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:55.742 align:middle line:90%
because population was smaller.

00:19:55.742 --> 00:19:57.200 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, you
get the picture, is

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.090 align:middle line:84%
that the total
number of mutations

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:02.360 align:middle line:84%
in any one of this that
are new since that new--

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:06.390 align:middle line:84%
since 5,000 generations ago
will be on the order of 10

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:12.380 align:middle line:84%
to the fourth in your body, not
all of them doing good for you.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:15.097 align:middle line:84%
And for each of those
rare mutations--

00:20:15.097 --> 00:20:17.555 align:middle line:84%
they might have a frequency of
about 10 to the minus five--

00:20:17.555 --> 00:20:21.670 align:middle line:90%


00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:26.018 align:middle line:84%
there will be about 10 to
the fourth people on Earth

00:20:26.018 --> 00:20:28.060 align:middle line:84%
that will share that very
rare mutation with you.

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:30.842 align:middle line:84%
10 to the minus 5
sounds very rare,

00:20:30.842 --> 00:20:32.800 align:middle line:84%
but when you multiply it
by six billion people,

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:35.140 align:middle line:84%
there are a lot of people
who share that with you.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:36.990 align:middle line:90%
It's a new mutation.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:37.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:38.980 align:middle line:90%
So the take-home is--

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:42.340 align:middle line:84%
and this is from
this reference here.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:45.700 align:middle line:84%
High genomic
deleterious mutations

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:49.180 align:middle line:90%
accumulate over these 4,000--

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:51.100 align:middle line:84%
some large number
of generations.

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.800 align:middle line:84%
And they would confound
linkage methods

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:55.840 align:middle line:84%
and they would
confound assumptions

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:58.120 align:middle line:84%
that say that the
common alleles--

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:02.190 align:middle line:90%
common alleles are causative.

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:02.700 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:04.950 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we've done
an association study.

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:07.200 align:middle line:84%
Either we've done it
on one gene, picked

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:09.480 align:middle line:84%
one out of the hat
like CCR5, or we've

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:12.060 align:middle line:84%
done it on a full genome
survey, 40,000 genes,

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:15.120 align:middle line:90%
all the alleles we know of.

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:18.180 align:middle line:84%
In order to do the latter,
we had to have a big patient

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:19.870 align:middle line:90%
population.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.120 align:middle line:90%
But let's say we've done that.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:25.020 align:middle line:84%
Now we want to prove that
that association is--

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:26.850 align:middle line:84%
that great statistic
that we got out,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:28.740 align:middle line:84%
if we have a large enough
patient population,

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:30.990 align:middle line:90%
is still just a statistic.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:35.040 align:middle line:84%
What will constitute proof is
after we find the association,

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.550 align:middle line:84%
we make a copy of that
mutation, isolated away

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:41.100 align:middle line:84%
from the three million
other polymorphisms that

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.870 align:middle line:84%
are floating around
in your body,

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:45.390 align:middle line:90%
and we do some kind of test.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:48.510 align:middle line:84%
Ideally, we would
make an isogenic pair

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:51.450 align:middle line:84%
of humans that only differ
by this one mutation.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:58.980 align:middle line:84%
That is not generally considered
medically feasible or ethical.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.590 align:middle line:84%
But somehow or another, you
can do it on human cells

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:07.110 align:middle line:84%
or you can do it in a
mammalian model system.

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:08.880 align:middle line:84%
But you have to make
something that's

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.580 align:middle line:84%
close to isogenic, a
copy of this mutation,

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.800 align:middle line:84%
and show that it has a
phenotype that makes sense.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.070 align:middle line:84%
And then, just to make
sure you haven't introduced

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:20.310 align:middle line:84%
other mutations, the
really careful scientists

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:23.610 align:middle line:84%
will then revert that one
polymorphism and show now

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.070 align:middle line:84%
you no longer have
that phenotype.

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:27.030 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:31.480 align:middle line:84%
So let's walk
through an example.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:35.990 align:middle line:84%
Here's the third
example in this lecture

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:38.790 align:middle line:84%
of a very specific
allele where we've

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.010 align:middle line:84%
shown the molecular
basis of it, and it's

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:43.158 align:middle line:84%
the second example
where it's not coding,

00:22:43.158 --> 00:22:45.450 align:middle line:84%
and it's the second example
where it's going to repeat.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:47.160 align:middle line:90%
So this is just to--

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:48.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm not giving you a
random sample here.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:53.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm specifically biasing this
towards very interesting genes,

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:57.240 align:middle line:84%
very interesting traits
that are associated

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:00.150 align:middle line:84%
with non-coding
repetitive alleles.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:03.750 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the trait
that got the attention

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:06.870 align:middle line:84%
of these researchers-- it
isn't necessarily proven

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:09.240 align:middle line:90%
that this is the relationship.

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:10.890 align:middle line:84%
But the association
that was studied

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:15.240 align:middle line:84%
was between anxiety-related
traits, anxiety,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.840 align:middle line:84%
and a polymorphism in the
serotonin transporter.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.140 align:middle line:90%
This is a science paper.

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.540 align:middle line:84%
Now the next step
in this is to--

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:26.820 align:middle line:90%
OK, that was found associated.

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:33.330 align:middle line:84%
There were lots of other alleles
randomly being moved around,

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:35.070 align:middle line:90%
because these are humans.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:39.432 align:middle line:84%
We have no control over who
mates with who, or very little.

00:23:39.432 --> 00:23:42.210 align:middle line:90%


00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.190 align:middle line:90%
So you get what you're given.

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:46.020 align:middle line:84%
You can do the survey
as best you can.

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:50.610 align:middle line:84%
But now in order to move it
towards a mechanistic basis

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:54.300 align:middle line:84%
and a proof of introducing
just this one point mutation.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:58.050 align:middle line:84%
Now what is the mutation
found in this case of anxiety

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:00.790 align:middle line:84%
relationship in this
serotonin transporter?

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:05.130 align:middle line:84%
It's upstream of the first
RNA encoding axon one.

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:09.080 align:middle line:84%
It is a 44 base pair deletion
in a repetitive region,

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:13.110 align:middle line:84%
in a region which might be
responsible for promoting

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:14.760 align:middle line:90%
transcription.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:18.690 align:middle line:84%
You have the short and the long
alleles, just like in the CCR5.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.450 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it's in a
putative promoter element.

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:25.020 align:middle line:84%
And when you make this
construct and you hook it up

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:30.970 align:middle line:84%
to a luciferase enzymatic
activity in vitro,

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:33.090 align:middle line:84%
in cultured cells so
you don't actually

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:36.300 align:middle line:84%
have to construct
a mutant human,

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:38.490 align:middle line:84%
you can now see the
long allele always

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:41.820 align:middle line:84%
produces higher
levels of expression

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:42.870 align:middle line:90%
than the short allele.

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:48.090 align:middle line:84%
And these little error bars
on top of each of the measures

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:51.720 align:middle line:84%
show your statistical measure
of standard deviation,

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.915 align:middle line:90%
as we did in the first lecture.

00:24:54.915 --> 00:25:00.150 align:middle line:84%
And if the black mean, which
is the height of the bar,

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.293 align:middle line:84%
is different from the white
mean by more than a couple

00:25:03.293 --> 00:25:05.460 align:middle line:84%
of these standard deviations,
which is the root mean

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:08.700 align:middle line:84%
square of the standard
deviations of each

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:12.790 align:middle line:84%
of the measures, then you call
it statistically significant.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:14.790 align:middle line:84%
And that's what these
triple stars mean.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:17.160 align:middle line:84%
It's the statistical
shorthand for this

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:20.220 align:middle line:84%
is statistically significant
at the cutoff that we're using.

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:21.900 align:middle line:90%
Say, 5%.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.760 align:middle line:84%
Now in this case, it's
kind of showing off

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.510 align:middle line:84%
because every one of
them is statistically

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:29.550 align:middle line:90%
significant by two different--

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:31.110 align:middle line:84%
completely different
assays here.

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:33.270 align:middle line:84%
But the point is
this does not prove

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:39.540 align:middle line:84%
that anxiety is meaningfully
associated with this.

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:44.580 align:middle line:84%
But it does prove that this
repetitive polymorphism

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:52.830 align:middle line:84%
is causally capable of
differential transcription

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.650 align:middle line:90%
levels for a reporter gene.

00:25:55.650 --> 00:26:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So by introducing it into
a clean cellular system,

00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:06.420 align:middle line:84%
you can test at least
part of the mechanism that

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:08.790 align:middle line:84%
might be involved in the
association that originally

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:11.520 align:middle line:90%
got your attention.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.490 align:middle line:84%
Now as you can
see, we're getting

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:21.150 align:middle line:84%
into a fairly mature and fairly
new phase of human genetics.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.860 align:middle line:84%
Some of the concepts
that are used here

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:25.440 align:middle line:84%
will be useful in a
variety of other systems

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:28.860 align:middle line:84%
where we have relatively little
control over the genetics.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:33.510 align:middle line:84%
But certainly in humans,
there's a very large need.

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:36.240 align:middle line:84%
And where this
happened historically

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.090 align:middle line:84%
is with Mendelian linkage,
which would involve

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:43.950 align:middle line:84%
very large families with
a complete pedigree,

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:47.160 align:middle line:84%
multi-generation,
great-grandfathers and mothers,

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:50.775 align:middle line:84%
all the way down, hundreds
of people in a family.

00:26:50.775 --> 00:26:53.400 align:middle line:84%
And that's the problem, because
there aren't that many diseases

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:54.775 align:middle line:84%
for which you have
large families

00:26:54.775 --> 00:26:56.670 align:middle line:84%
with simple Mendelian
inheritance.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:59.010 align:middle line:84%
Common diseases tend to
be much more complicated,

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:01.920 align:middle line:84%
involving multiple
genes simultaneously

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.280 align:middle line:90%
and small families.

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:09.000 align:middle line:84%
Then there's linkage
disequilibrium,

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:14.633 align:middle line:84%
which depends on
these common alleles

00:27:14.633 --> 00:27:16.800 align:middle line:84%
where the population has
gone through a particularly

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:20.640 align:middle line:84%
small bottleneck and where
your common allele is

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:23.400 align:middle line:84%
a pretty good distance away
from the causative one.

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:25.980 align:middle line:84%
But it allows you to
map it into a ballpark,

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:31.200 align:middle line:84%
and then you hunker down and
do the more expensive testing

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.960 align:middle line:84%
of hypotheses for all and
sequencing and looking

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:35.688 align:middle line:84%
for potentially
causative alleles.

00:27:35.688 --> 00:27:37.980 align:middle line:84%
Once you find a potentially
causative allele, something

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.000 align:middle line:84%
that looks suspicious,
maybe something

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.330 align:middle line:84%
that's in a coding region
that it's conserved--

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:45.510 align:middle line:84%
that's the first priority,
despite all the counterexamples

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:46.780 align:middle line:90%
I've given.

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:48.010 align:middle line:90%
And then you go ahead.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:49.802 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is that
you're at the mercy

00:27:49.802 --> 00:27:51.677 align:middle line:84%
of the recombination
that might have occurred

00:27:51.677 --> 00:27:54.480 align:middle line:84%
in that population, but you get
to very interesting populations

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:58.770 align:middle line:84%
like people of African
American descent

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.650 align:middle line:84%
where the population
is very old, hasn't

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:04.350 align:middle line:84%
passed through a
population bottleneck.

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:06.870 align:middle line:84%
The useful linkage
disequilibrium

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:10.320 align:middle line:84%
is on a couple of kilobases
rather than hundreds

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.510 align:middle line:84%
of kilobases, so it
actually is hard to find

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:13.830 align:middle line:90%
things that are linked.

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:16.410 align:middle line:84%
That is to say, insists
but not causatives.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:19.380 align:middle line:90%
Insists to the causative allele.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:22.020 align:middle line:84%
Instead, you have to look
directly for causative alleles.

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.840 align:middle line:84%
And that's where-- now you look
for common causative alleles.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:27.765 align:middle line:90%


00:28:27.765 --> 00:28:29.890 align:middle line:84%
This has the problem that
we've been talking about,

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:34.860 align:middle line:84%
which is that maybe common
alleles aren't often causative.

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:36.330 align:middle line:90%
They've been selected out.

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.400 align:middle line:84%
So then you get into
the scenario, what if we

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:39.930 align:middle line:90%
wanted to look at all alleles?

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:43.770 align:middle line:84%
Well, theoretically, there's
nothing wrong with that.

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:45.450 align:middle line:90%
It'd be great.

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:47.070 align:middle line:84%
We could do the
association studies.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:50.310 align:middle line:84%
We'd have to have fairly
large populations.

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:52.650 align:middle line:84%
But you saw it was linear
with an exponential increase

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:55.710 align:middle line:84%
in the number of hypotheses, and
you could rank those hypotheses

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:59.280 align:middle line:84%
by a priori likelihood and
so forth, but it's expensive.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:02.820 align:middle line:84%
And so the discussion
now has to include

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:07.110 align:middle line:84%
a little bit of discussion
of the new technologies that

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:10.470 align:middle line:84%
might make this less expensive,
and a discussion of how we got

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:12.900 align:middle line:84%
the first genome and how
the new technologies might

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.580 align:middle line:90%
be a little different.

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:17.070 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to do this
in the context of computation

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:19.810 align:middle line:84%
in the sense of, how do we
deal with random and systematic

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:20.310 align:middle line:90%
errors?

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:23.370 align:middle line:84%
As we plan our
strategy for getting

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:26.640 align:middle line:84%
each of our genomes, personal
genomes sequence for $1,000

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:30.850 align:middle line:84%
or so, how do we choose
which technology to pursue?

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:34.050 align:middle line:84%
Which ones have the
lowest intrinsic, random,

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.350 align:middle line:90%
and systematic errors?

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:37.110 align:middle line:84%
So we needed to study
the first genome

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:41.190 align:middle line:84%
to see, what would we mean by
random and systematic errors?

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:44.790 align:middle line:84%
How many people here know what
the difference is already?

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:47.370 align:middle line:90%


00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:47.890 align:middle line:90%
A few.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:48.390 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.720 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, whether you do or
not, it's good for the soul

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:54.420 align:middle line:84%
to go through some
real examples of this.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:56.160 align:middle line:84%
A random mutation
is something which

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:59.040 align:middle line:84%
occurs every time you
do the experiment.

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:01.680 align:middle line:90%
You get a different error.

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:06.240 align:middle line:84%
And a systematic
one is something

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.560 align:middle line:84%
where if you do it over
and over the same way,

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:10.920 align:middle line:84%
you'll get the same
error or a simple class

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:15.510 align:middle line:84%
of errors occurring more
than once, over and over.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:20.045 align:middle line:84%
So for sequencing, the process
involves picking something

00:30:20.045 --> 00:30:21.420 align:middle line:84%
that you want to
sequence-- we'll

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:22.950 align:middle line:90%
call it a clone or a template.

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:24.810 align:middle line:84%
Then actually doing
the sequencing

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:27.900 align:middle line:84%
and then assembling
this into a meaningful

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.690 align:middle line:90%
interpreted sequence.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:34.500 align:middle line:84%
And this is an
example where we might

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:41.370 align:middle line:90%
have chosen these big clones.

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:43.920 align:middle line:84%
In other words, you might
fragment the genome up randomly

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:46.140 align:middle line:84%
into large clones of
100 kilobases called

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:48.690 align:middle line:84%
bacterial artificial
chromosomes, for example.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:51.150 align:middle line:84%
And then that's all
random, and so there's

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:52.920 align:middle line:90%
a shotgun of that scale.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:55.840 align:middle line:84%
And then we break it up
randomly into smaller pieces,

00:30:55.840 --> 00:30:57.480 align:middle line:84%
which provide us with
little sequences.

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.940 align:middle line:84%
And then in the computer,
we assemble these by methods

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:04.410 align:middle line:84%
that we'll discuss in
the next lecture, which

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:07.600 align:middle line:84%
can take even fairly different
sequences than assemble them.

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:11.040 align:middle line:84%
In this case, we're talking
about very similar sequences.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.823 align:middle line:84%
And then you assemble the little
sequences into big sequences,

00:31:13.823 --> 00:31:15.240 align:middle line:84%
and you assemble
the big sequences

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:17.910 align:middle line:84%
into even bigger sequences, and
then you have the whole thing.

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:21.378 align:middle line:84%
But you can see there's a lot
of opportunity for error here.

00:31:21.378 --> 00:31:22.920 align:middle line:84%
We make it look
simple in this slide,

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:25.980 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to talk about
random and systematic errors

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:28.020 align:middle line:90%
in a moment.

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:29.610 align:middle line:84%
Where did we get
those sequences,

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:32.340 align:middle line:84%
those little sequences
that we want to assemble?

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:34.600 align:middle line:84%
We're going to go through
a few methods in a moment,

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:37.140 align:middle line:84%
but the most common
one by far I think

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:40.380 align:middle line:84%
that generates over
90% of the human genome

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:42.420 align:middle line:84%
sequence in the
last couple of years

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:47.130 align:middle line:84%
is capillary electrophoresis
of fluorescently labeled

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:49.320 align:middle line:90%
polymerase terminated products.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.390 align:middle line:84%
When you electrophoresis
things, you're

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:57.375 align:middle line:84%
separating DNA fragments,
which are n nucleotides long,

00:31:57.375 --> 00:32:00.630 align:middle line:84%
from a DNA fragment which is
n plus one nucleotides long.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.030 align:middle line:84%
That's a very subtle
difference as n gets big.

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:05.280 align:middle line:84%
As n gets big, it
gets harder and harder

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.395 align:middle line:90%
to separate n from n plus one.

00:32:07.395 --> 00:32:10.320 align:middle line:84%
As it does so, you start
getting all kinds of errors.

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:14.310 align:middle line:84%
The total number of errors here
in the lower left-hand corner

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:16.740 align:middle line:84%
is the number of insertions
plus the number of deletions

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.310 align:middle line:84%
plus the number of
substitutions plus n, which

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:23.640 align:middle line:90%
is an abbreviation for no call.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:27.000 align:middle line:84%
It means the software felt
that it was so close that it

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.000 align:middle line:90%
couldn't call it at all.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:28.830 align:middle line:90%
It just said it's n.

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.413 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
it's an A, C-- it

00:32:30.413 --> 00:32:34.390 align:middle line:84%
doesn't know whether it's an A,
C, G, or T. Calls it a no call.

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:36.660 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look
through this table,

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:43.680 align:middle line:84%
it shows on the vertical axis
for each of these six bar

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:47.820 align:middle line:84%
charts is as you go up
on the vertical axis,

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:50.250 align:middle line:84%
you go from very
short reads where

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:52.950 align:middle line:84%
it's easy to separate n from
n plus one electrophoretically

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:55.500 align:middle line:84%
to very long ones where
you start accumulating

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:58.680 align:middle line:84%
insertions deletion
errors, insertion deletion

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:00.240 align:middle line:90%
substitution, and n.

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:02.170 align:middle line:90%
They all go up with length.

00:33:02.170 --> 00:33:06.390 align:middle line:84%
And so you can think of that
as a random error superimposed

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:10.380 align:middle line:84%
on the systematic error of if
you always do the experiments,

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:13.620 align:middle line:84%
the same base pair is always
at the end of your run,

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.260 align:middle line:84%
it's going to always have
a higher random error rate.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:19.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is kind of a combination
of random and systematic error.

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:22.680 align:middle line:84%
So let's go through
some examples here.

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.770 align:middle line:84%
We have just
isolating the template

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:31.350 align:middle line:84%
to prepare it for sequencing,
there are systematic errors.

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:33.270 align:middle line:84%
If you have certain
kinds of repeats,

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:36.360 align:middle line:84%
long, inverted repeats, or
certain kinds of restriction

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.150 align:middle line:84%
elements that the bacteria
doesn't like or likes

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.960 align:middle line:84%
to chew up, then you
won't get the clone.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:45.510 align:middle line:84%
And that would plague the early
part of the Genome Project,

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:48.122 align:middle line:84%
is you'd keep trying again
and again the same way,

00:33:48.122 --> 00:33:49.830 align:middle line:84%
and you just wouldn't
get certain clones.

00:33:49.830 --> 00:33:51.360 align:middle line:84%
It's as if there
was a hole there.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:52.943 align:middle line:84%
You know that there's
something there,

00:33:52.943 --> 00:33:55.020 align:middle line:90%
but you couldn't clone it.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:58.560 align:middle line:84%
Sequencing-- hairpins can
form in the single strand

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:00.060 align:middle line:84%
of nucleic acids
where you're trying

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:02.100 align:middle line:90%
to separate n from n plus one.

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:06.990 align:middle line:84%
And those hairpins then take
out the gel electrophoresis

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:10.800 align:middle line:84%
and make it seem like it's much
smaller than it actually is.

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.560 align:middle line:84%
Tandem repeats cause
a problem in all three

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:16.980 align:middle line:90%
of these stages in sequencing.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:19.530 align:middle line:84%
You get some
polymerase stuttering

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:21.719 align:middle line:90%
and you get little artifacts.

00:34:21.719 --> 00:34:24.810 align:middle line:84%
In assembly repeats,
you're assembling

00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:27.310 align:middle line:90%
by sequence alignment.

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:28.770 align:middle line:84%
If you have a
repeat, the repeat's

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:31.110 align:middle line:84%
going to align just
as well because it's

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:33.215 align:middle line:84%
a repeat within the
genome as a repeat

00:34:33.215 --> 00:34:35.340 align:middle line:84%
due to experimental repeating,
and so the alignment

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:36.900 align:middle line:90%
can be off.

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:39.150 align:middle line:84%
The errors that you got
from these earlier steps

00:34:39.150 --> 00:34:40.860 align:middle line:90%
make it hard to assemble.

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:42.570 align:middle line:90%
Polymorphisms look like errors.

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:44.580 align:middle line:84%
Chimeric clones means
you've got things

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:52.010 align:middle line:84%
that have been misassembled
early on here, and so on.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:59.902 align:middle line:84%
When we do this random
selection of big clones

00:34:59.902 --> 00:35:02.110 align:middle line:84%
and random selection of
little clones for sequencing,

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:04.060 align:middle line:90%
we want to know when to quit.

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:05.890 align:middle line:84%
Now you could say,
well, we're going

00:35:05.890 --> 00:35:07.450 align:middle line:84%
to quit once we get
it all assembled,

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:10.120 align:middle line:84%
but you need to accumulate
a certain amount of data

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:12.070 align:middle line:84%
in advance before you
try to assemble it.

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:15.820 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
various calculations

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:17.110 align:middle line:90%
as to when to quit.

00:35:17.110 --> 00:35:19.580 align:middle line:84%
And this is related to
the Poisson distribution,

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:20.650 align:middle line:90%
but it's not exactly.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, one of the
studies done in 1988

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:28.990 align:middle line:84%
made some poor
assumptions about--

00:35:28.990 --> 00:35:32.320 align:middle line:84%
remember, we mentioned
in the first lecture

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:36.310 align:middle line:84%
the assumptions of the
Poisson distribution

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:38.170 align:middle line:84%
that make it an
approximation-- when

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:42.310 align:middle line:84%
it becomes an approximation
to a more formal distribution

00:35:42.310 --> 00:35:43.180 align:middle line:90%
like the binomial.

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:46.420 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, that approximation,
you should be getting,

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:48.130 align:middle line:84%
as you get higher
and higher coverage,

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:52.240 align:middle line:84%
meaning more and more
experimental repeats,

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:54.460 align:middle line:84%
you'll eventually
fill all the gaps.

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:58.630 align:middle line:84%
And that means you'll get
100% complete coverage,

00:35:58.630 --> 00:36:00.910 align:middle line:84%
and it should asymptotically
approach that.

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:03.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you use the
Poisson incorrectly,

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:08.080 align:middle line:84%
as authors did in 1988, you
basically go off to infinity.

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:12.800 align:middle line:84%
You get 200% coverage, which
is physically impossible.

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:15.400 align:middle line:84%
But both an earlier
study, which they ignored,

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:17.830 align:middle line:84%
and a more recent
study got it right.

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:21.010 align:middle line:84%
And these are slightly
different measures,

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:24.250 align:middle line:90%
but they both converge on 100%.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:26.230 align:middle line:84%
And I urge you to
look at that if you're

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:29.560 align:middle line:84%
designing an experiment
with simple formula.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:32.530 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if you want
to design the experiment more

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.080 align:middle line:84%
explicit, you eventually
get to a point

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:37.810 align:middle line:84%
where a simple analytic
formula won't do,

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:39.160 align:middle line:90%
and you have to do Monte Carlo.

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:41.290 align:middle line:84%
We treated this
in the first class

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:44.830 align:middle line:84%
of analytic versus numeric
differentiable equations

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:46.340 align:middle line:90%
and then many other cases.

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:49.380 align:middle line:84%
The simulation you
want to do is beyond--

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.280 align:middle line:90%
is too hard to do analytically.

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:56.020 align:middle line:84%
So Gene Myers set up-- just
listed all the things he

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:57.790 align:middle line:90%
thought would be--

00:36:57.790 --> 00:37:02.470 align:middle line:84%
could affect the ability to
assemble a genome sequence.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:07.990 align:middle line:84%
The read length and types of
repeats, and all this stuff

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:09.160 align:middle line:90%
was simulated.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:13.510 align:middle line:84%
And he cranked the simulation on
real human genome size projects

00:37:13.510 --> 00:37:16.210 align:middle line:84%
and came to the conclusion
that you could do a shotgun

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:18.460 align:middle line:90%
assembly of a mammalian genome.

00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:20.320 align:middle line:84%
A company called
Celera was formed.

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:24.520 align:middle line:84%
Gene Myers was hired
as the computer guru.

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:27.250 align:middle line:84%
They put together a
large stable of computers

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:31.060 align:middle line:84%
and they started doing this on
the human and the mouse genome.

00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:33.640 align:middle line:84%
The human genome was in the
end not done by this method,

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:36.550 align:middle line:84%
but the mouse genome was, and
it was a pretty good assembly.

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:39.670 align:middle line:84%
So the Drosophila genome
missing the Drosophila repeats

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:41.360 align:middle line:90%
was also done by this method.

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:43.870 align:middle line:84%
So this is scaled
very gracefully

00:37:43.870 --> 00:37:49.480 align:middle line:84%
from the first shotgun sequence,
which was on a four kV plasmid

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:52.510 align:middle line:90%
to mammalian size genomes.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:54.670 align:middle line:84%
No mammalian genome is
completely sequenced,

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.440 align:middle line:84%
so we can't really
declare victory.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:59.950 align:middle line:84%
But the sort of simulation
that he did here

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:01.105 align:middle line:90%
has played out very nicely.

00:38:01.105 --> 00:38:03.760 align:middle line:90%


00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:05.980 align:middle line:84%
Right here in Boston,
there will be--

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:07.480 align:middle line:84%
when we start
thinking of the future

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:09.160 align:middle line:84%
of sequencing
technology, we would all

00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:12.040 align:middle line:84%
like-- this is almost
uncontroversial as

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:16.090 align:middle line:84%
to how much we desire that the
genome not cost $3 billion,

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:17.920 align:middle line:90%
but it costs $1,000.

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:20.110 align:middle line:84%
And now there are
a number of people

00:38:20.110 --> 00:38:26.980 align:middle line:84%
who are taking very definite
steps to get us to that point.

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:30.040 align:middle line:84%
To understand systematic
and random errors that

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:32.080 align:middle line:84%
can occur in these
steps, let's take

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:35.560 align:middle line:84%
this dideoxy gel electrophoresis
we've been talking about here.

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:37.660 align:middle line:84%
You have four different
colored terminators.

00:38:37.660 --> 00:38:40.750 align:middle line:84%
These is where the polymerase
can't go any further

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:43.532 align:middle line:84%
because there's a
blocking group here,

00:38:43.532 --> 00:38:44.740 align:middle line:90%
which is fluorescent labeled.

00:38:44.740 --> 00:38:46.720 align:middle line:84%
And if the template
on the far left

00:38:46.720 --> 00:38:50.320 align:middle line:84%
here is ready to accept
an A, you'll get an A.

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:54.070 align:middle line:84%
And this n and n minus
one will separate

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:57.040 align:middle line:84%
on this electrophoresis and
the four colors will give you

00:38:57.040 --> 00:39:00.700 align:middle line:84%
this four-colored pattern
where the intensity reflects

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:03.670 align:middle line:84%
good termination
at that position,

00:39:03.670 --> 00:39:05.830 align:middle line:84%
and you can basically
go along reading it.

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:07.990 align:middle line:90%
G, C, G, G, A, T.

00:39:07.990 --> 00:39:09.760 align:middle line:84%
Now this is in the
well-behaved part.

00:39:09.760 --> 00:39:13.510 align:middle line:84%
An example of a systematic error
that you get in this extension

00:39:13.510 --> 00:39:17.320 align:middle line:84%
occurs here in the upper
right-hand corner of slide 40.

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Now you've got-- because
of one of these hairpins

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:20.968 align:middle line:84%
that I mentioned
before, you've got

00:39:20.968 --> 00:39:24.700 align:middle line:84%
a pile-up of seven nucleotides
all at the same position.

00:39:24.700 --> 00:39:26.242 align:middle line:84%
A completely
alternative method that

00:39:26.242 --> 00:39:27.700 align:middle line:84%
doesn't involve
gel electrophoresis

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:29.920 align:middle line:84%
is called pirA
sequencing, and this

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:34.480 align:middle line:84%
is an equivalent that can be
done with fluorescent addition.

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:36.760 align:middle line:84%
Instead of separating
them in time

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.550 align:middle line:84%
by their size in
electrophoresis,

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:43.120 align:middle line:84%
in time you ask serially,
do you want an A here?

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:45.400 align:middle line:84%
If yes, then you
get a little green--

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:49.070 align:middle line:84%
you get a little
peek of representing

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:51.740 align:middle line:84%
the release of pyrophosphate
or the incorporation

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:53.055 align:middle line:90%
of fluorescent A.

00:39:53.055 --> 00:39:55.130 align:middle line:84%
And then you ask,
do you want a T,

00:39:55.130 --> 00:39:56.450 align:middle line:90%
and so forth, and you go along.

00:39:56.450 --> 00:40:00.230 align:middle line:84%
And each signal means
yes to the answer

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:02.550 align:middle line:84%
that it's ready for
that particular base.

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:04.490 align:middle line:84%
And you can see this
doesn't have any problems

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:06.050 align:middle line:90%
with this hairpin region.

00:40:06.050 --> 00:40:08.690 align:middle line:90%


00:40:08.690 --> 00:40:10.190 align:middle line:84%
So when you have a
systematic error,

00:40:10.190 --> 00:40:11.898 align:middle line:84%
you have to change
the method that you're

00:40:11.898 --> 00:40:13.670 align:middle line:90%
using fairly radically.

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:17.435 align:middle line:84%
The opposite strand or a
completely different method.

00:40:17.435 --> 00:40:20.230 align:middle line:90%


00:40:20.230 --> 00:40:24.280 align:middle line:84%
If you have-- early on,
we had huge differences

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:27.760 align:middle line:84%
in intensity of the
fluorophores and new enzymes

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:31.150 align:middle line:84%
and new fluorophores were
developed by Tabor and Matthews

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:32.450 align:middle line:90%
and Glaser, and so forth.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:36.580 align:middle line:84%
And this was a huge advance
in making everything uniform

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:39.320 align:middle line:84%
and eliminating
systematic errors.

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:41.950 align:middle line:84%
So the sort of things that are
on the horizon that hopefully

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:45.040 align:middle line:84%
will be discussed
tomorrow in Boston,

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:51.100 align:middle line:84%
we've talked about this short
number of base extensions

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:53.080 align:middle line:84%
like power
sequencing, the longer

00:40:53.080 --> 00:40:56.980 align:middle line:84%
extensions in capillary
arrays, which, capillary arrays

00:40:56.980 --> 00:40:58.690 align:middle line:84%
are changing from
low-end capillaries

00:40:58.690 --> 00:41:01.780 align:middle line:90%
into microfabricated chips.

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:04.015 align:middle line:84%
I'll show an example of a
mass spec in just a moment.

00:41:04.015 --> 00:41:07.150 align:middle line:90%


00:41:07.150 --> 00:41:08.980 align:middle line:84%
Sequencing by
hybridization on arrays

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:13.030 align:middle line:84%
will illustrate as a prelude,
as an example of Affymetrix

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:17.860 align:middle line:90%
technology in this slide.

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:18.370 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:41:18.370 --> 00:41:22.750 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here, this is
mainly for re-sequencing.

00:41:22.750 --> 00:41:25.780 align:middle line:84%
Some of the ways of reducing
the cost of sequencing

00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:28.480 align:middle line:84%
the human genome will not
apply to sequencing brand

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:29.260 align:middle line:90%
new genomes.

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:30.970 align:middle line:84%
But still, we need
to pursue them,

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:33.880 align:middle line:84%
because this may be the way that
we get the $1,000 human genome,

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:36.260 align:middle line:90%
even if we don't get other ones.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:39.730 align:middle line:84%
And here, you know the
sequence, except there's

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:42.540 align:middle line:84%
a possibility that there could
be a polymorphism at any base.

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:45.640 align:middle line:84%
Could be any single base
substitution at any base.

00:41:45.640 --> 00:41:47.335 align:middle line:84%
And so you don't
necessarily know

00:41:47.335 --> 00:41:49.690 align:middle line:84%
in advance what the common
ones are or the rare ones,

00:41:49.690 --> 00:41:52.420 align:middle line:84%
but you do know the
canonical sequence.

00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:58.750 align:middle line:84%
And so at every position, you'll
make a 25-mer oligonucleotide

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:00.520 align:middle line:84%
which will bind to a
fluorescently labeled

00:42:00.520 --> 00:42:03.910 align:middle line:84%
version of your genome,
or a piece of your genome.

00:42:03.910 --> 00:42:06.760 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually developed
for HIV re-sequencing

00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:08.170 align:middle line:90%
by Affymetrix.

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:11.620 align:middle line:84%
And at this middle
position, you'll

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:18.785 align:middle line:84%
put in all four possible
substitutions, T, G, C, or A.

00:42:18.785 --> 00:42:24.870 align:middle line:84%
And you'll consider
each possible template.

00:42:24.870 --> 00:42:26.750 align:middle line:84%
So if you have the
template, which is-- let's

00:42:26.750 --> 00:42:28.167 align:middle line:84%
say these are the
two alleles that

00:42:28.167 --> 00:42:31.820 align:middle line:84%
occur in a human population
or in your sample.

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:35.180 align:middle line:84%
You can have this sequence and
then all the variations on it,

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:39.453 align:middle line:84%
stepping along, changing T,
G, C, A for the first base,

00:42:39.453 --> 00:42:41.370 align:middle line:84%
the second base, the
third base, and so forth,

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:42.287 align:middle line:90%
till you hit this one.

00:42:42.287 --> 00:42:44.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is where the
real polymorphism occurs.

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:47.030 align:middle line:84%
And you could have either
this as the context

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:49.310 align:middle line:90%
or this as the context.

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:52.310 align:middle line:84%
And this is schematic And
this is the real data.

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:54.170 align:middle line:84%
These are real data
down here where

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:57.140 align:middle line:84%
you have the context of
the A allele or the context

00:42:57.140 --> 00:42:58.190 align:middle line:90%
of the C allele.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:01.640 align:middle line:84%
You can have the homozygotes
or the heterozygotes.

00:43:01.640 --> 00:43:04.703 align:middle line:84%
And in the homozygous A,
you can see it lights up

00:43:04.703 --> 00:43:06.620 align:middle line:84%
the best, the best
hybridization when you have

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:07.828 align:middle line:90%
the A in the middle position.

00:43:07.828 --> 00:43:09.380 align:middle line:84%
The middle position
is most sensitive

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:11.250 align:middle line:90%
to hybridization changes.

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:16.280 align:middle line:84%
And for the C, you have
it in the C row here.

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:18.410 align:middle line:84%
And remember, you're
changing all the bases

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:19.160 align:middle line:90%
in every position.

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:20.077 align:middle line:90%
One, two, three, four.

00:43:20.077 --> 00:43:23.150 align:middle line:84%
This is the one where the middle
base of the middle position

00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:24.780 align:middle line:90%
is in the right context.

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:27.110 align:middle line:84%
So this is in the A
context and the C context.

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:30.470 align:middle line:84%
And in the heterozygote, you
get both the A and the C.

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:32.720 align:middle line:84%
And so this has
been done on HIV.

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:34.730 align:middle line:84%
It's been done on
BRCA1, on mitochondria.

00:43:34.730 --> 00:43:37.640 align:middle line:84%
And now they've applied
it with whole wafers

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:39.630 align:middle line:90%
to the entire human genome.

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:44.630 align:middle line:84%
And this probably costs on
the order of $3 million or so.

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:46.400 align:middle line:84%
Mass spectrometry is
another way that's

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:48.525 align:middle line:84%
used, probably not for
sequencing the whole genome,

00:43:48.525 --> 00:43:51.590 align:middle line:84%
but it's used for single
nucleotide polymorphisms.

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:56.660 align:middle line:84%
This costs on the order
of $0.50 per polymorphism.

00:43:56.660 --> 00:44:00.650 align:middle line:84%
If there's three million
of them in your genome,

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:03.743 align:middle line:90%
that's a lot of $0.50.

00:44:03.743 --> 00:44:07.130 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:09.680 align:middle line:84%
And here's what it looks
like when you read it out.

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:11.690 align:middle line:84%
It's really just
like electrophoresis.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:17.120 align:middle line:84%
You can now separate
an addition of an A

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:22.535 align:middle line:84%
from an addition of a G. And the
difference in mass between an A

00:44:22.535 --> 00:44:23.930 align:middle line:84%
and a G, this is
even more subtle

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:25.847 align:middle line:84%
than the difference of
an n and an n plus one.

00:44:25.847 --> 00:44:27.805 align:middle line:84%
This is just a difference
between an A and a C.

00:44:27.805 --> 00:44:28.910 align:middle line:90%
It's detectable in this.

00:44:28.910 --> 00:44:31.580 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it's detectable
and quantitative enough

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:32.912 align:middle line:90%
that you can pool samples.

00:44:32.912 --> 00:44:35.120 align:middle line:84%
This is a bit of a stunt,
but it's an important stunt

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:40.100 align:middle line:84%
to show that this is really
a very precise method, albeit

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:42.530 align:middle line:90%
still fairly expensive.

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:45.320 align:middle line:84%
Now just in closing,
I want to give you

00:44:45.320 --> 00:44:49.580 align:middle line:84%
the simplest possible
example of how we

00:44:49.580 --> 00:44:51.020 align:middle line:90%
can search through sequences.

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:53.103 align:middle line:84%
Next week, we'll give you
a much more rigorous way

00:44:53.103 --> 00:44:54.728 align:middle line:84%
that you can look
through the sequences

00:44:54.728 --> 00:44:57.090 align:middle line:84%
with very extensive differences
between the sequences.

00:44:57.090 --> 00:45:00.110 align:middle line:84%
But here, the theme
of today's talk

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:02.840 align:middle line:84%
is the subtle
polymorphism differences

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:04.370 align:middle line:90%
that occur between you and me.

00:45:04.370 --> 00:45:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And here, one different--
so generally, you're

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.480 align:middle line:90%
looking for exact matches.

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:11.450 align:middle line:84%
And a good way to look
for exact matches is--

00:45:11.450 --> 00:45:14.570 align:middle line:84%
good ways are hashing, suffix
arrays, and suffix trees

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:16.500 align:middle line:84%
where basically
in each of these,

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:21.110 align:middle line:84%
you're looking for using
a word, a word either

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:24.830 align:middle line:84%
that's built up and stored
from the end, the suffix,

00:45:24.830 --> 00:45:27.530 align:middle line:84%
one letter at a
time, or it's a chunk

00:45:27.530 --> 00:45:29.150 align:middle line:90%
that you might have as a hash.

00:45:29.150 --> 00:45:31.292 align:middle line:90%
And you make up a lookup table.

00:45:31.292 --> 00:45:32.750 align:middle line:84%
And the size of
that lookup table--

00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:34.542 align:middle line:84%
it's a trade-off between
speed of searching

00:45:34.542 --> 00:45:35.810 align:middle line:90%
and the size of the table.

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:39.378 align:middle line:84%
The size is going to be-- if
the word is n nucleotides long,

00:45:39.378 --> 00:45:40.670 align:middle line:90%
it's going to be four to the n.

00:45:40.670 --> 00:45:42.253 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be the
storage space you

00:45:42.253 --> 00:45:45.560 align:middle line:84%
have to put on disk
or RAM, RAM if you

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:46.910 align:middle line:90%
want it to be a fast search.

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:50.390 align:middle line:84%
And so 16 is the magic number,
sort of in the ballpark

00:45:50.390 --> 00:45:52.490 align:middle line:84%
for a human genome,
because four to the 16th

00:45:52.490 --> 00:45:55.400 align:middle line:84%
is four billion sequences
you can represent.

00:45:55.400 --> 00:45:56.670 align:middle line:90%
But it's a huge table.

00:45:56.670 --> 00:45:59.330 align:middle line:84%
You have to have a table of
four billion times however

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:04.130 align:middle line:84%
many bytes you need to
store the positions.

00:46:04.130 --> 00:46:06.470 align:middle line:84%
Typically, about four
bytes of storage.

00:46:06.470 --> 00:46:08.060 align:middle line:84%
If you cut back on
this a little bit,

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:09.518 align:middle line:84%
you'll end up with
collisions where

00:46:09.518 --> 00:46:13.310 align:middle line:84%
you'll have two things that
have the same hash or suffix.

00:46:13.310 --> 00:46:14.930 align:middle line:90%
If you make it--

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:16.353 align:middle line:84%
and that's if you
make it smaller.

00:46:16.353 --> 00:46:17.270 align:middle line:90%
It'll take less space.

00:46:17.270 --> 00:46:18.680 align:middle line:84%
If you make it
bigger, it'll take

00:46:18.680 --> 00:46:21.020 align:middle line:90%
a ridiculous amount of RAM.

00:46:21.020 --> 00:46:25.070 align:middle line:84%
And then here's a
kind of whimsical--

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:27.500 align:middle line:84%
another example of pearl
where you not only want

00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:30.110 align:middle line:84%
to find all the mutations
here at a very high density--

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:33.080 align:middle line:84%
a ridiculously high density,
not one particular base

00:46:33.080 --> 00:46:34.618 align:middle line:90%
for every few base pairs.

00:46:34.618 --> 00:46:37.160 align:middle line:84%
You not only want to find them,
but you want to correct them.

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:40.130 align:middle line:84%
And here, the pearl
does the substitution.

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:43.765 align:middle line:84%
And after gene therapy,
everybody walks out happy.

00:46:43.765 --> 00:46:44.265 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:44.265 --> 00:46:46.360 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]